<h3 id="user-1">User:</h3>
<p>how to host a littleFedi instance on a raspberry pi behind tailscale</p>

<h3 id="assistant-1">Assistant:</h3>
<p>To host a <strong>littleFedi</strong> instance on a Raspberry Pi behind Tailscale, you must overcome the major hurdle of typical Fediverse hosting: other instances need to reach your server over standard public HTTPS, but Tailscale hides your Pi from the public internet.</p>

<p>To solve this, littleFedi includes a built-in feature called <strong>littleMesh</strong>. This mechanism allows your private instance to securely peer and federate through a public “lighthouse” proxy without needing public DNS records, an open router port, or a public IP address.</p>

<p>Here is how to set up littleFedi on your Pi with Tailscale for private client access while keeping federation active via littleMesh.</p>

<hr />

<h4 id="step-1-install-tailscale-on-the-raspberry-pi">Step 1: Install Tailscale on the Raspberry Pi</h4>
<p>You need Tailscale active on your Pi so your phones, laptops, and Mastodon clients can connect to it securely from anywhere.</p>

<ol>
  <li>SSH into your Raspberry Pi.</li>
  <li>Run the official Tailscale installation script:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>curl <span class="nt">-fsSL</span> https://tailscale.com ¦ sh
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>Authenticate and bring the network interface up:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>tailscale up
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>Copy your Pi’s Tailscale IP address for future reference:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>tailscale ip <span class="nt">-4</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<h4 id="step-2-download-and-install-littlefedi">Step 2: Download and Install littleFedi</h4>
<p>Because littleFedi compiles down to a single, static Go binary with an embedded SQLite database, installation takes seconds.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Download the correct ARM binary for your Raspberry Pi architecture from the official littleFedi release page.</li>
  <li>Move the binary into your execution path and make it executable:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo mv </span>littlefedi /usr/local/bin/
<span class="nb">sudo chmod</span> +x /usr/local/bin/littlefedi
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<h4 id="step-3-run-the-interactive-setup">Step 3: Run the Interactive Setup</h4>
<p>littleFedi includes an onboarding tool to configure your instance and automatically request/configure the <strong>littleMesh</strong> federation tunnel.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Initialize the setup wizard:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>littlefedi init
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>When prompted by the interactive command line:
    <ul>
      <li>Select <strong>Lighthouse / littleMesh</strong> mode when asked about network topology.</li>
      <li>Provide the network address or choose the embedded SQLite database profile.</li>
      <li>If running on a low-end Pi (like a Pi Zero), toggle <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">low_power = true</code> in the configuration file to optimize database locks and memory caching.</li>
    </ul>
  </li>
</ol>

<h4 id="step-4-configure-the-web-interface-listener">Step 4: Configure the Web Interface Listener</h4>
<p>To ensure you can log into your instance using a Mastodon mobile app or web browser over your Tailscale network, change the listening interface.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Open your generated littleFedi configuration file (usually <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">config.toml</code> or similar).</li>
  <li>Set the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">listen</code> address to explicitly bind to your <strong>Tailscale IP address</strong> (from Step 1) or to all local interfaces:
    <div class="language-toml highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="py">listen</span> <span class="p">=</span> <span class="s">"0.0.0.0:8080"</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>(Optional) If you want a smooth interface without manual port typing, install a lightweight reverse proxy like Caddy on the Pi to map incoming Tailscale traffic on port 80/443 over to your littleFedi port.</li>
</ol>

<h4 id="step-5-automate-with-systemd">Step 5: Automate with systemd</h4>
<p>Create a background service file so littleFedi automatically boots and recovers when your Pi restarts.</p>

<ol>
  <li>Create a service file:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>nano /etc/systemd/system/littlefedi.service
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>Paste the configuration text below (adjusting paths to match where your config file is stored):
    <div class="language-ini highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nn">[Unit]</span>
<span class="py">Description</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">littleFedi Server</span>
<span class="py">After</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">network.target tailscaled.service</span>

<span class="nn">[Service]</span>
<span class="py">Type</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">simple</span>
<span class="py">User</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">pi</span>
<span class="py">WorkingDirectory</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">/home/pi/littlefedi</span>
<span class="py">ExecStart</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">/usr/local/bin/littlefedi --config /home/pi/littlefedi/config.toml</span>
<span class="py">Restart</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">always</span>

<span class="nn">[Install]</span>
<span class="py">WantedBy</span><span class="p">=</span><span class="s">multi-user.target</span>
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>Enable and start the service:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>systemctl daemon-reload
<span class="nb">sudo </span>systemctl <span class="nb">enable </span>littlefedi
<span class="nb">sudo </span>systemctl start littlefedi
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<hr />

<h4 id="step-6-connect-your-clients">Step 6: Connect Your Clients</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Web UI:</strong> Turn on Tailscale on your laptop, type your Pi’s Tailscale IP address (e.g., <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">http://100.x.y.z:8080</code>) into your browser, and log in.</li>
  <li><strong>Mobile Apps:</strong> Use any Mastodon-compatible app (Mona, Ivory, Ice Cubes) while connected to your Tailnet. Enter your localized Tailscale instance URL when the app requests your server domain.</li>
</ul>

