<h3 id="user-2">User:</h3>
<p>Provide the configuration blocks to set up Caddy as a local HTTPS reverse proxy using Tailscale’s automatic SSL certificates.</p>

<h3 id="assistant-2">Assistant:</h3>
<p>To set up Caddy Server as a local reverse proxy with automatic Tailscale HTTPS certificates, you don’t need any complex SSL configuration block. Caddy automatically communicates with the local <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">tailscaled</code> daemon to request and renew the TLS certificates for any domain ending in <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.ts.net</code>.</p>

<h4 id="step-1-grant-caddy-access-to-tailscale">Step 1: Grant Caddy Access to Tailscale</h4>
<p>By default, the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">caddy</code> user cannot access the Tailscale socket file to request certificates. You must tell the Tailscale daemon to allow the <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">caddy</code> system user to read its state.</p>

<p>Run this command on your Raspberry Pi:</p>
<div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>tailscale up <span class="nt">--operator</span><span class="o">=</span>caddy
</code></pre></div></div>

<h4 id="step-2-find-your-tailnet-magicdns-domain">Step 2: Find Your Tailnet MagicDNS Domain</h4>
<p>You need the exact, unique domain string that Tailscale assigned to your Raspberry Pi. Get it by executing:</p>
<div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>tailscale status <span class="nt">--peers</span><span class="o">=</span><span class="nb">false</span>
</code></pre></div></div>
<p><em>Look for your device’s Full Domain Name (e.g., <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">raspberrypi.tail1234.ts.net</code>).</em></p>

<h4 id="step-3-configure-the-caddyfile">Step 3: Configure the Caddyfile</h4>
<p>Open or create your <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">/etc/caddy/Caddyfile</code>:</p>
<div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>nano /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
</code></pre></div></div>

<p>Replace the contents with the block below. Make sure to swap out your actual <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.ts.net</code> address and verify the port matches your <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">littlefedi</code> instance config (assuming port <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">8080</code>):</p>

<pre><code class="language-caddy"># Replace with your actual device domain from Step 2
raspberrypi.tail1234.ts.net {
    # Route all traffic securely to your local littleFedi instance
    reverse_proxy localhost:8080
    
    # Optional headers to ensure proper Fediverse activity headers pass through
    header {
        X-Forwarded-Proto https
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h4 id="step-4-apply-and-restart-services">Step 4: Apply and Restart Services</h4>
<ol>
  <li>Validate your Caddy syntax formatting:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code>caddy validate <span class="nt">--config</span> /etc/caddy/Caddyfile
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
  <li>Reload the Caddy service configuration to fetch the certificate from Tailscale:
    <div class="language-bash highlighter-rouge"><div class="highlight"><pre class="highlight"><code><span class="nb">sudo </span>systemctl reload caddy
</code></pre></div>    </div>
  </li>
</ol>

<h4 id="troubleshooting">Troubleshooting</h4>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Handshake/Connection Errors:</strong> Ensure you have explicitly enabled <strong>HTTPS Certificates</strong> in your Tailscale Admin Console under the DNS settings menu.</li>
  <li><strong>Port Conflicts:</strong> Ensure <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">littlefedi</code> is listening on a local port like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">127.0.0.1:8080</code> and <strong>not</strong> trying to bind to port <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:80</code> or <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">:443</code>, as Caddy needs absolute control over those public-facing web ports.</li>
</ul>

