<h3 id="assistant-3">Assistant:</h3>
<p>You can use <strong>either</strong>, but the choice radically changes how your instance connects to the rest of the Fediverse.</p>

<p>Here is how the domain name works depending on which path you choose:</p>

<h4 id="option-1-using-your-tailscale-domain-tsnet">Option 1: Using your Tailscale domain (<code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.ts.net</code>)</h4>
<p>If you use the Tailscale domain (e.g., <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">your-pi.tail1234.ts.net</code>), you get automatic, zero-configuration HTTPS via Caddy.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>How you connect:</strong> You type <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">https://ts.net</code> into your browser or mobile app.</li>
  <li><strong>The Catch:</strong> Other public Fediverse servers (like Mastodon or Bluesky bridges) <strong>cannot</strong> see or talk to a <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">.ts.net</code> domain because it only exists inside your private Tailscale network. You will be completely isolated unless you utilize littleFedi’s built-in <strong>littleMesh</strong> lighthouse feature to tunnel the traffic out.</li>
</ul>

<h4 id="option-2-using-your-own-custom-domain-eg-yournamecom">Option 2: Using your own custom domain (e.g., <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">://yourname.com</code>)</h4>
<p>You can absolutely use your own custom domain name, which is highly recommended if you want a clean, professional username like <code class="language-plaintext highlighter-rouge">@you@yourname.com</code>.</p>
